1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,370
CairoFlow turns one recorded traffic video into tracked vehicles, turning counts, and an approximate 3D twin of the junction.

2
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,821
20.33 percent.

3
00:00:12,271 --> 00:00:16,897
That's how far our count of vehicles entering the junction was from the published reference.

4
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:20,941
Our limit was 20 percent, and we fixed it before the held-out read.

5
00:00:21,391 --> 00:00:25,875
So in this fifteen-minute study, CairoFlow refuses to show you those counts.

6
00:00:27,025 --> 00:00:31,013
Cities re-time signals and rebuild junctions from traffic counts.

7
00:00:31,073 --> 00:00:34,024
But a vision model won't tell you when its count is wrong.

8
00:00:34,174 --> 00:00:38,376
So we built a pipeline where every headline figure shows its gate status.

9
00:00:39,226 --> 00:00:42,923
One overhead camera: Lankershim Boulevard, Los Angeles.

10
00:00:43,023 --> 00:00:48,071
The footage isn't redistributed, so you see the stored tracks from the local detector and tracker.

11
00:00:48,221 --> 00:00:53,911
Asked for "car", SAM 3.1 followed at most 16 vehicles, and none that arrived later.

12
00:00:54,011 --> 00:00:58,363
So on one 60-second window, we gave it our tracked boxes as its prompts.

13
00:00:58,463 --> 00:01:07,675
It agreed with 9,900 of 10,858 observed rows. A consistency check, not independent confirmation.

14
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:12,379
Its own boxes replaced 586 rows our tracker had filled in.

15
00:01:13,059 --> 00:01:17,838
The fifteen-minute record drives a 3D twin of the junction, on one shared clock.

16
00:01:17,938 --> 00:01:19,996
Click any vehicle to read its record.

17
00:01:20,096 --> 00:01:22,510
Approximations are labelled, on screen.

18
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:31,552
Counted tracks become turning movements, set beside the published reference. Across all twelve: 801 against 911.

19
00:01:32,432 --> 00:01:33,708
Then comes the gate.

20
00:01:33,858 --> 00:01:36,309
Before the test, we committed seven limits.

21
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:45,073
We tuned the detector and tracker on alternating minutes of footage, then scored the held-out minutes once, against the published NGSIM trajectories.

22
00:01:45,133 --> 00:01:49,213
That's agreement, not accuracy: the reference comes from the same video.

23
00:01:49,443 --> 00:01:52,375
Pooled across the held-out minutes, six checks passed.

24
00:01:52,655 --> 00:01:56,009
Entering counts came in at 20.33 percent error.

25
00:01:56,069 --> 00:01:57,358
The limit was 20.

26
00:01:58,608 --> 00:02:00,952
A miss of a third of a percentage point.

27
00:02:01,142 --> 00:02:02,445
We didn't move the line.

28
00:02:02,595 --> 00:02:06,874
So the fifteen-minute study shows no entering-flow figure. Only the reason.

29
00:02:07,024 --> 00:02:13,066
A withheld value is absent, not zero. The data contract won't let a failed metric carry a number.

30
00:02:13,916 --> 00:02:18,596
Muse Spark reads that evidence, and writes hypotheses for a person to test.

31
00:02:18,696 --> 00:02:24,130
Its reply schema has no numeric field, and code checks that every citation exists.

32
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:29,907
All three replies passed. Here's one hypothesis, word for word, labelled as model output.

33
00:02:32,157 --> 00:02:40,794
For the decision, which used published NGSIM trajectories rather than our camera output, SUMO tested three signal changes Muse proposed.

34
00:02:40,894 --> 00:02:46,283
In simulation, every one made something worse, and the simulator's baseline isn't validated.

35
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:49,622
So CairoFlow recommends nothing. And shows you why.

36
00:02:50,872 --> 00:02:56,629
CairoFlow. Decision support for traffic engineers. It tells you what it knows, and what it doesn't.
